<th id="S4.T3.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Agent (Sequential)</span></th>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">66.1 (31.0 – 107.0)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">32.9 (22.3 – 48.0)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">57.5 (7.0 – 105.0)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">39.5 (10.1 – 130.5)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">19.9 (5.0 – 74.0)</span></td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.1.5.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T3.2.1.5.7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">45.3 (15.8 – 101.6)</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3. </span>Participants’ utterance counts and average length per utterance (in words; word counts computed using whitespace delimitation), with means and ranges, across clinician- and agent-led interviews, separated by role (interviewer, patient, informant).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Symptom elicitation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2. Symptom elicitation ‣ 4. Result ‣ Designing and Evaluating a Conversational Agent for Early Detection of Alzheimer’s Disease and Related Dementias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> displays the count of symptom labels for the 24 paired agent and clinician interviews, and Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2. Symptom elicitation ‣ 4. Result ‣ Designing and Evaluating a Conversational Agent for Early Detection of Alzheimer’s Disease and Related Dementias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S4.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 4.2. Symptom elicitation ‣ 4. Result ‣ Designing and Evaluating a Conversational Agent for Early Detection of Alzheimer’s Disease and Related Dementias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> summarizes key measures of symptom elicitation from the agent-led interview transcripts. When focusing only on symptoms that were clearly present or absent in both the agent and clinician interviews for all paired transcripts (n=24 pairs), overall diagnostic performance showed sensitivity of 80.9% and specificity of 90.8% across all paired transcripts (n=24). Sequential prompting (n=12) achieved higher sensitivity than full-script prompting (n=12) (86.4% vs 75.4%) but comparable specificity (91.7% vs 90.3%). See Table 2 for details including confidence intervals. However, per symptom sensitivity and specificity (19 symptoms with at least five cases that were present or absent in both transcripts) had wide variability due to low sample size for each symptom ranging from 25-100% and 50-100% respectively, most with confidence intervals of 40% or greater.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
