</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Socio-technical Considerations in Designing Conversational Agents for Early Detection of ADRD</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our study shows that conversational agents for early detection of ADRD need to be designed as socio-technical systems, meaning that their effectiveness depends not only on reliable language processing but also on how they fit the communication styles of older adults, particularly those with cognitive impairment, and the dynamics of multi-party interaction. Prior research has found that older adults often treat voice assistants in different ways—e.g., as friendly companions, as simple appliances, or as fragile listeners—and these perceptions shape how they talk to the system <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pradhan et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In our study, many participants began by speaking slowly and over-articulating, which can be understood as a way of helping the agent “hear better.” This behavior can be understood as a user-led repair strategy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moore et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, anticipating possible recognition failures. Prior work has shown that such adjustments are common when people interact with conversational agents, as users actively compensate for expected system limitations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Luger and Sellen, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Porcheron et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cuadra et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. Such behaviors indicate that older adults are not merely passive recipients but active participants in sustaining the interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Salomé et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. System design can build on this by explicitly scaffolding such strategies, for example by providing clear listening indicators that reassure users their effort is being understood <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahmad et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. In our study, we observed that participant behavior seemed to change the most when the agent gave concise summaries of what the participant had stated and/or attempted to clarify participant responses, resulting in the participant then speaking in a more natural and conversational manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition, multi-party interviews in our study involved not just the agent and a single user, but a triad of bot, patient, and often informant. This setting created unique challenges for turn-taking and role clarity, echoing broader findings that voice assistants in shared spaces often face difficulties with overlapping talk and ambiguous addressees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Porcheron et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng-Schneider et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. To reduce confusion, the system used explicit visual cues to signal when the agent was speaking or listening, and it was designed to recognize the presence of both patient and informant, addressing the patient first while inviting the informant when needed. For example, one agent utterance was phrased as:</p>
<blockquote id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">“I understand this might be difficult to discuss. Let me try to make this simpler. To the person accompanying the patient, could you tell me roughly when you first noticed any changes in your loved one’s memory or behavior? Even a general time frame like ‘about two years ago’ would be helpful.” (agent utterance, interview with P313)</span></p>
</blockquote>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Beyond these technical strategies, role management was further complicated by the fact that, in our current setup, patients and informants were interviewed together in the same room. Prior work in caregiving contexts has similarly noted that co-present arrangements can suppress or shape what caregivers are willing to share <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bartle et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thakkar et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruitenburg et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In our study, informants sometimes withheld details or appeared cautious in front of the patient—for example, agreeing with the patient’s account even when they seemed to disagree, or holding back corrective information to avoid conflict. These same informants would often reveal conflicting information in the clinician interview, and indeed, some informants confided with study team members that they did not want to upset their loved one during the agent interview, while they felt this must be done in the clinician interview to ensure the clinician received the information they needed for diagnosis and management. To address this limitation, our planned phone call-based system (see Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1. System Overview ‣ 3. Method ‣ Designing and Evaluating a Conversational Agent for Early Detection of Alzheimer’s Disease and Related Dementias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>) separates the two interviews, allowing informants to contribute more candidly without the constraints of co-presence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>Toward Clinically Useful Conversational Agents</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Current cognitive impairment screening tools, while valuable for initial detection, create a significant downstream burden on an already overwhelmed healthcare system. Every positive screen requires a comprehensive clinical evaluation to confirm the result and rule out false positives, then conduct the time-intensive diagnostic evaluation needed to identify the specific cause of cognitive symptoms and initiate time-sensitive treatment if appropriate. Our agent-led interview system is designed to address this gap by functioning as a diagnostic support tool rather than a screener. In our study, agent-led interviews systematically covered a wide variety of complex ADRD symptom domains and elicited symptoms with comparable clarity to dementia specialists, suggesting that such agents hold great promise in serving as structured diagnostic interview assistants that gather comprehensive symptom histories for subsequent clinical evaluation. A notable finding was that conversational agents more consistently covered our pre-defined list of ADRD symptoms compared to dementia specialists during diagnostic interviews. However, this comprehensive coverage cannot be interpreted as evidence of superior interview performance. The dementia specialists in our study were blinded to our symptom list and may prioritize different symptoms based on their clinical experience and each patient’s unique presentation, reflecting expected practice variation in the field. As we enroll more participants, we will investigate whether the additional symptoms identified by the agent represent clinically relevant information that specialists might miss. These findings highlight the potential for complementary strengths: agents could provide systematic baseline coverage of all diagnostic domains, while clinicians apply their expertise to probe deeper into the most relevant symptoms for each individual case and provide nuanced interpretation necessary for accurate diagnosis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Participants’ experience with the agent interviews provided additional insights into their potential clinical utility. Participants highlighted the agent’s <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">patience</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">thoroughness</span> as positive qualities, echoing prior findings that conversational AI can impose less psychological burden than traditional assessments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Igarashi et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Patients and informants provided longer responses to the agent than to the clinician as measured by word count per utterance, potentially due to patient and informant awareness of the time limitations of their clinic appointments. Participants noted the agent could be <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“really useful to people who are waiting a long time to see a doctor”</span> and felt it was <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">“very thorough”</span> in capturing their histories. Such perceptions indicate that conversational agents can not only gather clinically relevant information but also foster trust, a prerequisite for adoption in sensitive healthcare contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mahmood et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Design Implications and Future Directions</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the specific findings of our study, several broader lessons emerge for patient-centered interaction design in health contexts. First, our results reinforce that conversation design is not a neutral technical choice, but a key determinant of user experience and clinical utility. As presented in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3. Agent Interaction Behavior ‣ 4. Result ‣ Designing and Evaluating a Conversational Agent for Early Detection of Alzheimer’s Disease and Related Dementias" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">sequential prompting</span> showed clear advantages over <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full-script prompting</span>: it made the interviews feel more natural, encouraged follow-up questions, and was preferred by more participants. But it also came with challenges—when the <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moved on to next topic</span> signal failed, the agent sometimes skipped content, lowering topic coverage, or became stuck on a topic, and in one case the interview became so long that the participant gave up. These issues are technically solvable, for example, by setting an upper bound on turns per topic to prevent the agent from stalling, or by restricting consecutive topic shifts to avoid unintentional skipping. The broader lesson, however, is that conversational agents for health must balance richness and completeness of elicitation with practical limits on time and participant effort. In addition, the strategy by which the agent is instructed through prompting plays a crucial role in ensuring conversation quality.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Before these tools can be deployed clinically, several validation challenges must be addressed. While the overall sensitivity and specificity of symptom elicitation was encouraging, the per symptom sensitivity and specificity was quite variable with large confidence intervals due to a low sample size. Clinical utility will require establishing which symptoms can be reliably gathered by conversational agents, ideally with very high sensitivity allowing clinicians to trust "absent" symptoms in order to focus time on "present" symptoms and/or clarifying ambiguous ones. In addition, further investigation is needed to understand if the comprehensive agent-led interviews lead to more accurate, diagnoses, improved management decisions, and/or enhanced clinical efficiency. If future research establishes clinical utility, integrating these tools into clinical workflows will require deliberate design choices and monitoring for safety. Rather than delivering raw transcripts, the system’s output should be concise, clinician-ready summaries (a "History of Present Illness") that highlight the symptoms most relevant to the patient’s interaction. These summaries will need to entail a presentation of symptoms with examples and quotes to capture nuance, a timeline of onset and change, clear markers of not-discussed or ambiguous symptoms, and a note on informant–patient disagreements. Such structured summaries can support clinical care by flagging cases that require expedited evaluation, help clinicians target their questions more effectively, and shorten visits without sacrificing thoroughness. This would also relieve clinician note writing burden, a strong factor in supporting adoption. In under-resourced settings, even a brief one-page summary could provide individuals who are often overlooked in routine care with a pathway to timely intervention. Real-time monitoring systems would be needed for the conversational agents to automatically flag deviations from therapeutic objectives or identify emergencies. Human intervention triggers are essential for high-risk scenarios, particularly suicidal ideation or medical emergencies, necessitating hybrid models combining automated agents with clinician oversight.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Moving forward, the next step is to enroll more participants and expand the research beyond in-clinic studies. We plan to develop and deploy the system in remote, phone-call based settings, where it can reach people who cannot easily attend specialty clinics and where patients and informants can be interviewed separately. Such settings will introduce new challenges—unlike the quiet, structured environment of a clinic, phone-based interviews take place in everyday spaces where distractions or interruptions may occur, but where participants might also feel more comfortable and candid. Some participants may have difficulty finding a private space, which is necessary when discussing sensitive health information. At the same time, they provide an opportunity to evaluate clinical utility under more natural conditions, offering insight into how conversational agents can function effectively beyond urban specialty clinics and support broader accessibility to ADRD diagnosis.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our study demonstrates that conversational agents can meaningfully support the early detection of ADRD by conducting semi-structured, voice-based interviews with older adults and their informants. Recent advances in LLMs have made such systems increasingly feasible, yet their effectiveness ultimately depends on alignment with the communication styles of older adults and the dynamics of multi-party interaction. In our evaluation, agent-led interviews elicited clinically relevant narratives across diverse symptom domains with a level of clarity comparable to dementia specialists, and participants highlighted the agent’s patience and thoroughness as positive qualities. At the same time, the study revealed key socio-technical considerations, including how older adults adapted their speech to support the system and how informant disclosure was constrained by co-presence with the patient. Looking ahead, expanding to remote, phone-based settings and scaling participant enrollment will be critical for assessing clinical utility under real-world conditions. Taken together, these findings underscore both the promise and the design considerations required for developing clinically useful conversational agents in dementia care.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.acknowledgements1" class="ltx_acknowledgements">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_acknowledgements">Acknowledgements.</h6>
We sincerely thank the patients and their families for their participation in this study.

</div>
</section>
<section id="bib" class="ltx_bibliography">
